      <article class="article-body">

        <h2 id="overview">What is Liraglutide?</h2>
        <p>Liraglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist developed by Novo Nordisk — the same company behind semaglutide. Approved in 2010 for type 2 diabetes (Victoza) and 2014 for weight management (Saxenda), liraglutide paved the way for the GLP-1 revolution.</p>
        <p>While newer agents have surpassed it in weight loss efficacy, liraglutide remains important for patients who need extensive long-term safety data, or who prefer the faster washout of a daily injection.</p>

        <h3>Two Brand Names, One Active Ingredient</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="comparison-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Indication</th><th>Form</th><th>Max Dose</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Brand"><strong>Victoza</strong></td>
                <td data-label="Indication">Type 2 Diabetes</td>
                <td data-label="Form">Daily injection</td>
                <td data-label="Max Dose">1.8 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Brand"><strong>Saxenda</strong></td>
                <td data-label="Indication">Weight Management</td>
                <td data-label="Form">Daily injection</td>
                <td data-label="Max Dose">3.0 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Unlike weekly semaglutide and tirzepatide, liraglutide requires daily injection. This means faster washout if side effects are problematic, but less convenience.</div>

        <h2 id="clinical-results">Clinical Trial Results</h2>
        <h3>SCALE Trials (Weight — Saxenda)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>SCALE Obesity:</strong> Average weight loss of 8% over 56 weeks (vs. 2.6% placebo).</li>
          <li>About 33% of participants achieved ≥10% body weight loss.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>LEADER Trial (Cardiovascular — Victoza)</h3>
        <p>Demonstrated liraglutide reduced major adverse cardiovascular events by 13% in patients with type 2 diabetes and high CV risk — one of the first diabetes drugs with proven heart benefits.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Context:</strong> While 8% weight loss is lower than semaglutide (~15%) or tirzepatide (~22%), a 5–10% loss is associated with significant improvements in blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, and sleep apnoea.</div>

        <div class="inline-cta">
          <h3>Interested in Liraglutide?</h3>
          <p>Discuss whether Victoza or Saxenda is right for you with a licensed provider.</p>
          <a href="#" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check Your Eligibility →</a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="dosing">Dosing Schedule</h2>
        <h3>Saxenda (Weight Management)</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="comparison-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Week</th><th>Dose</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Week">Week 1</td>
                <td data-label="Dose">0.6 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Week">Week 2</td>
                <td data-label="Dose">1.2 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Week">Week 3</td>
                <td data-label="Dose">1.8 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Week">Week 4</td>
                <td data-label="Dose">2.4 mg/day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Week">Week 5+</td>
                <td data-label="Dose">3.0 mg/day (maintenance)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3>Victoza (Diabetes)</h3>
        <p>Start at 0.6 mg/day for one week, then increase to 1.2 mg/day (standard), with optional increase to 1.8 mg/day for additional control. Can be injected any time of day.</p>

        <h2 id="side-effects">Side Effects</h2>
        <h3>Very Common (&gt;10%)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Nausea</strong> (~39%)</li>
          <li><strong>Diarrhoea</strong> (~21%)</li>
          <li><strong>Constipation</strong> (~19%)</li>
          <li><strong>Vomiting</strong> (~16%)</li>
          <li><strong>Headache</strong> (~14%)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Serious (Rare)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Pancreatitis</li>
          <li>Gallbladder disease</li>
          <li>Thyroid C-cell tumours (boxed warning)</li>
          <li>Acute kidney injury</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Advantage of daily dosing:</strong> If you experience intolerable side effects, liraglutide clears your system within 2–3 days. Weekly injectables take 4–5 weeks to fully wash out.</div>

        <h2 id="cost">Cost &amp; Insurance</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Victoza:</strong> ~$900–$1,000/month</li>
          <li><strong>Saxenda:</strong> ~$1,300–$1,400/month</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Insurance coverage for Victoza (diabetes) is generally better than for newer agents. Some providers consider liraglutide a good "starter" GLP-1 when insurance covers it more readily.</p>

        <div class="inline-cta">
          <h3>Compare Liraglutide Prices</h3>
          <p>Find the most affordable way to get Victoza or Saxenda from licensed providers.</p>
          <a href="#" rel="nofollow sponsored">Compare Prices →</a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="who">Who is Liraglutide Best For?</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Patients wanting a shorter-acting option</strong> — faster washout if side effects are problematic</li>
          <li><strong>Adolescents</strong> — Saxenda is FDA-approved for ages 12+ for weight management</li>
          <li><strong>Patients prioritising safety data</strong> — 15+ years of real-world post-marketing data</li>
          <li><strong>Cardiovascular risk patients</strong> — proven CV benefit from LEADER trial</li>
          <li><strong>Insurance-driven choices</strong> — often better covered than newer alternatives</li>
        </ul>

      </article>
